WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.304
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:08.944
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:15.716
Unit twenty-two: Environmental Philosophy. Today you will argue about nature using need not / must not.

4
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.610
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:23.183
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:24.832
Nature.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.777
Nature means the physical world, including living things apart from human creation.

8
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:34.393
Nature existed long before humanity wrote about it.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.276
Your turn. Say: Nature.

10
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:40.891
Nature.

11
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.918
Intrinsic value.

12
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:48.819
Intrinsic value means worth that something has in itself, regardless of its usefulness.

13
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.275
Many argue that a rainforest has intrinsic value.

14
00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:55.861
Your turn. Say: Intrinsic value.

15
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.131
Intrinsic value.

16
00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:01.954
Stewardship.

17
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.668
Stewardship means the responsible care of something entrusted to us.

18
00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:10.908
Stewardship treats the land as a trust, not a possession.

19
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:14.285
Your turn. Say: Stewardship.

20
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:17.949
Stewardship.

21
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:20.079
Anthropocentrism.

22
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:25.788
Anthropocentrism means the view that human beings are the centre of moral concern.

23
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:29.869
Anthropocentrism judges nature by its usefulness to people.

24
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:33.368
Your turn. Say: Anthropocentrism.

25
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:37.710
Anthropocentrism.

26
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.567
Ecology.

27
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:45.091
Ecology means the study of relationships between living things and their environment.

28
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:49.599
Ecology shows how a single species can shape a whole habitat.

29
00:01:50.183 --> 00:01:52.952
Your turn. Say: Ecology.

30
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:56.964
Ecology.

31
00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.136
Responsibility.

32
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.274
Responsibility means the duty to take care of something or answer for it.

33
00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:07.011
Responsibility grows with power.

34
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:10.548
Your turn. Say: Responsibility.

35
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.715
Responsibility.

36
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.020
Future generations.

37
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:21.235
Future generations means people who will live after us.

38
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.523
We owe future generations a livable planet.

39
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:28.199
Your turn. Say: Future generations.

40
00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:32.534
Future generations.

41
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:34.308
Justice.

42
00:02:35.317 --> 00:02:37.781
Justice means fair and morally right treatment.

43
00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:42.299
Environmental justice asks who bears pollution's costs.

44
00:02:42.883 --> 00:02:45.303
Your turn. Say: Justice.

45
00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.145
Justice.

46
00:02:49.933 --> 00:02:53.735
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:54.583 --> 00:02:57.639
Visal, why protect a swamp if nobody uses it?

48
00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:05.803
Because value need not depend on usefulness. Many philosophers argue that nature has intrinsic worth.

49
00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:09.392
But humans must come first, surely.

50
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:20.972
Anthropocentrists say so. Yet a rich life need not exclude concern for other species, and we must not assume that our interests are the only ones that matter.

51
00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:23.456
What are our duties, then?

52
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:35.515
We must not treat the earth as disposable. Stewardship means that we need not stop using nature, but we must use it as trustees for future generations.

53
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.550
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.417 --> 00:03:43.436
Grammar focus: Modal of necessity (need not and must not).

55
00:03:43.736 --> 00:03:44.970
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:47.927
Value need not depend on usefulness.

57
00:03:48.817 --> 00:03:51.257
We must not treat the earth as disposable.

58
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.520
Stewardship need not forbid all human use of nature.

59
00:03:56.467 --> 00:04:07.025
need not = it is not necessary (permitted but optional). must not = forbidden. Both take the bare infinitive: need not depend (never 'need not to depend').

60
00:04:07.783 --> 00:04:11.562
Be careful! Don't say: Value not need depend on usefulness.

61
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.324
Say: Value need not depend on usefulness.

62
00:04:16.624 --> 00:04:18.740
need not + bare infinitive.

63
00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:22.453
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.115
Does Nature Matter in Itself?.

65
00:04:25.415 --> 00:04:32.616
Environmental philosophy asks whether the natural world deserves moral concern for its own sake or merely for what it provides.

66
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:43.425
Anthropocentrism, the dominant tradition, assigns value only to human interests, so a forest is valuable if it supplies timber, medicine or recreation.

67
00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:53.318
Deep ecologists reply that value need not depend on usefulness: species and ecosystems may possess intrinsic value that humans must not ignore.

68
00:04:54.333 --> 00:05:03.055
Stewardship offers a middle path: we need not leave nature untouched, but we must not exhaust it, since we hold it in trust for future generations.

69
00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:11.070
Questions of justice follow, because the burdens of environmental damage seldom fall equally on rich and poor.

70
00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:16.389
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.505
Value blank depend on usefulness.

72
00:05:19.805 --> 00:05:23.981
A, need not. B, needs not to. C, must to not.

73
00:05:27.533 --> 00:05:29.684
The answer is A: need not.

74
00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.616
Worth that something has in itself is blank value.

75
00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:38.138
A, instrumental. B, intrinsic. C, economic.

76
00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.688
The answer is B: intrinsic.

77
00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.999
The view that humans are the centre of moral concern is blank.

78
00:05:48.299 --> 00:05:52.881
A, ecology. B, anthropocentrism. C, stewardship.

79
00:05:56.433 --> 00:05:59.315
The answer is B: anthropocentrism.

80
00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.217
Which means 'it is forbidden'?

81
00:06:02.517 --> 00:06:07.587
A, You need not touch it.. B, You must not touch it.. C, You do not need to touch it..

82
00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:13.888
The answer is B: You must not touch it..

83
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:19.778
According to the reading, stewardship says we need not leave nature untouched but must not…

84
00:06:20.078 --> 00:06:23.557
A, use it at all. B, exhaust it. C, study it.

85
00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.285
The answer is B: exhaust it.

86
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.338
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:37.309
Does nature have value beyond its usefulness to humans?

88
00:06:54.117 --> 00:06:55.814
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.114 --> 00:07:20.114
I believe it does. The value of a coral reef need not depend on whether it feeds us or attracts tourists. Living systems have a good of their own, and we must not assume that only human interests count. At the same time, we need not stop using nature altogether; we must simply do so responsibly. Our duties extend to future generations as well.

90
00:07:20.414 --> 00:07:22.053
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:28.110
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal of necessity (need not and must not).

92
00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:34.418
Today's value is environmental stewardship. Care for nature as a trust for future generations.

93
00:07:34.718 --> 00:07:37.846
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:38.146 --> 00:07:40.880
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

